# Reflex — the intelligence layer between your harness and the frontier model

> Give your agent a spinal cord.

**Reflex** is an open-source middleware layer that sits between any AI agent harness
(Hermes, Claude Code, Codex, LangChain, PydanticAI...) and expensive frontier-model calls.
It makes the thousands of small, repetitive decisions your agent makes every day —
which tool, which tier, safe to act, worth escalating — **fast, typed, calibrated, and nearly free**,
and it **trains itself on your own traffic** so it gets better every day you run it.

## Why

Every agent harness today pays frontier prices for spinal-reflex work:
- "Which tool should handle this?" — a 30ms decision, priced as an 8s LLM call
- "Is this loop stuck?" — a yes/no, priced by the token
- "Can I act without asking?" — a probability, delivered as prose

TypeSafe's Jev proved the *contract* (typed questions → calibrated probabilities).
Laya proved the *training recipe* (RLCD, proper scoring rules). Grep.ai's AgentRun proved
the *four-agent execution pattern*. **Reflex is the integrated, self-training, open version of
all three — built for any harness, any model backend, any GPU provider.**

## The layer stack (subsidiarity: every decision at the lowest competent layer)

```
L0  CODE      grants, safety spine, regexes, caches       — free, instant, owns ALL authority
L1  REFLEX    typed decision model (choice/score/noul)    — 30ms-2s, $0, CALIBRATED (ECE ≤ 0.10)
              → which tool first? needs full agent? stuck? compact? guardrail?
L2  HARNESS   four-agent execution (Research/Judge/Report/Operator) on a small local model
              — seconds, ~$0; Operator proposes only; grants-checked at L0
L3  FRONTIER  your big model. Only the genuinely hard ~2-5%.
```

A wrong low-layer decision costs at most what you pay today (L3 anyway).
A right one never wakes the frontier at all.

## What's in this repo

- `harness/` — the four-agent execution harness: Research (read-only, reads real files),
  Judge (verifies quotes, escalates hard cases), Report (renders, never re-decides),
  Operator (proposals only in v0.x; reversible-only execute path with L0 grant checks).
  Static grants file (`grants.json`) owns authority — no model can ever modify it.
- `runtime/` — the L1 loop: decision scorer (constrained-token scoring on a 1B backbone),
  shadow-capture + hindsight-labeling ticks, the decision ledger (one sqlite flywheel for
  every layer), skip-if-confident gateway hook.
- `training/` — the RLCD-style training scripts (proper scoring rules, balanced multi-task
  sampling), the nightly redeploy trainer with a **never-deploy-worse gate**, and the
  temperature-fitting calibration step.

## Honest numbers (ours, frozen evals, no cherry-picking)

| head | accuracy | ECE | baseline |
|---|---|---|---|
| skill-gate first_tool (13-way) | 83.6% | 0.067 | 44.5% majority |
| needs-full-agent (noul) | 93.6% | 0.064 | 90.9% majority |
| stuck-check (noul) | 99.6% | 0.000 | — |
| compaction keep/summarize/drop | 80.7% | 0.052 | ~40% |

Calibration (ECE) is the headline metric, not accuracy: a gate that can't be trusted at
its word is worse than no gate. We refuse to deploy any model that doesn't beat its
incumbent on EVERY metric — see the deploy gate.

## The flywheel

```
operate → capture decisions + hindsight outcomes → label honestly
        → nightly retrain (proper scoring rules) → deploy only if strictly better
        → more decisions stay cheap → operate more → repeat
```

Your ledger never leaves your machine. Nothing phones home. That's the point.

## Quick start

```bash
pip install -e .            # harness + runtime
# serve any small model (vllm example):
vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct --port 8000
# score a decision with a 1B reflex model on CPU:
echo "check the price of the RTX 4070" | python -m reflex.score
# run the four-agent harness (dry-run Operator):
python -m reflex.harness --task examples/task.json
```

Works with any OpenAI-compatible backend (local vllm, llama.cpp, or hosted APIs),
any harness (adapters for Hermes/Claude Code/Codex in `adapters/`),
any GPU provider for nightly training (RunPod/Lambda/your box — scripts are provider-agnostic).

## Status

Pre-1.0. Built and running in production at Teloplex (that's our own agent's nervous
system you're reading about). MIT licensed. Weights on HuggingFace (coming).

## Contributing

We want: harness adapters for more agents, label recipes for new decision types
(the honest-label discipline is strict: no deterministic labels, no incumbent-behavior
labels — hindsight outcomes only), and eval improvements. Read `docs/LABELING.md` first.

— Teloplex
